WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.119 --> 00:00:02.110
Researchers have set up a system

00:00:02.110 --> 00:00:04.950
that lets them see mutations in living cells

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:06.609
and follow the cells’ fates.

00:00:06.609 --> 00:00:08.080
Good, bad, or neutral---

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:10.640
spontaneous mutations are not easy to detect

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:12.160
in a living system.

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:14.629
But there are important unanswered questions

00:00:14.629 --> 00:00:16.760
about how mutations are distributed

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:18.510
and how common they are.

00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:19.970
This information makes it possible

00:00:19.970 --> 00:00:22.450
to use the mutation rate of organisms

00:00:22.450 --> 00:00:24.260
to look back in time to see when

00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:26.530
two related species diverged.

00:00:26.530 --> 00:00:27.530
In this view,

00:00:27.530 --> 00:00:30.660
the mother cells are along the top of a micro-fluidic
chip,

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:32.660
so-named because narrow channels

00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:36.059
control the liquid’s flow in very specific ways.

00:00:36.059 --> 00:00:37.750
The offspring bud off and

00:00:37.750 --> 00:00:39.980
expand into the tiny tubes,

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:42.160
each cell pinched off from its progenitor.

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:43.800
Newly made cells

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:46.760
are whisked away by liquid at the bottom of the tube.

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:48.180
Bacteria are red and

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:51.559
replication error machinery--markers for mutation-

00:00:51.559 --> 00:00:53.379
are the yellow dots.

00:00:53.379 --> 00:00:56.690
Researchers followed cells over 200 generations

00:00:56.690 --> 00:00:57.909
to look for mutations and

00:00:57.909 --> 00:00:59.860
the effects those mutations had.

00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:02.659
They were able to calculate the rate of deadly mutations --

00:01:02.659 --> 00:01:04.650
at about 1% --

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:06.330
much lower than expected.

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:09.200
They also found that mutations occur regularly

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:10.760
in individual cells--

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:12.640
they all get mutations

00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:14.140
at about pretty much the same rate,

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:16.030
regardless of stress level--

00:01:16.030 --> 00:01:18.380
and that mutations are less detrimental

00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:20.390
to the bacteria than expected.

00:01:20.390 --> 00:01:23.100
This new technique tracking mutations in single cells

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:25.280
could be used in cancer research or

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:28.400
to explore different mutation patterns in
different tissues.

